      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We are given two positive real numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) whose geometric mean and harmonic mean are \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\) respectively. We need to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nThe geometric mean of two numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{ab} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\\[\nab = 3\n\\]\n\nThe harmonic mean of two numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\frac{2ab}{a + b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(ab = 3\\) into the harmonic mean formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{a + b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nSimplifying the equation, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{a + b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\\[\n12 = 3(a + b)\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 3, we get:\n\\[\na + b = 4\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\nab = 3\n\\]\n\\[\na + b = 4\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this system by treating it as a quadratic equation. If \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the roots of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - (a+b)x + ab = 0\\), then the quadratic equation is:\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -4\\), and \\(c = 3\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 - 12}}{2} = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{4}}{2} = \\frac{4 \\pm 2}{2}\n\\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 + 2}{2} = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{4 - 2}{2} = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the two numbers are \\(a = 3\\) and \\(b = 1\\). The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1, 3}\n\\]",
